WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:03.330
Welcome to PyMOL 101 with Molecular Memory!

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:08.750
KP here, and today we’ll be learning all
about the different ways to measure the distance

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.620
between atoms in PyMOL.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:17.220
Examining distances between atoms is important
because it can tell us if a potential molecular

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.260
interaction does—or doesn’t exist.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.540
Today’s structure is 1A0o.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:29.580
Let’s load it as usual and display the sequence.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:35.800
This is actually segments of two interacting
proteins from bacteria, so let’s learn a

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:40.930
new PyMOL preset that models the interaction
of two proteins.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:45.760
Action, preset, protein interface.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:47.530
Nice.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:53.800
Chain A, the green chain, is part of a protein
that can cause a change the movement of bacterial

00:00:53.800 --> 00:01:01.890
flagella, and the cyan chain B is a segment
of a protein that activates the green chain.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:09.450
This protein duo helps bacteria move in response
to chemical signals in their environment.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:15.799
Proteins form when many amino acids undergo
dehydration reactions to link up together.

00:01:15.799 --> 00:01:20.070
These reactions are between an amine and a
carboxylic acid.

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:26.820
But, because proteins are chains, one end
will have an amine that didn’t react, and

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.159
the other will have a carboxylic acid that
didn’t react.

00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:37.530
The amine end is called the N-terminus and
the end with the unreacted carboxylic acid

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.920
is the C-terminus.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.939
It's easy to identify these in PyMOL.

00:01:42.939 --> 00:01:48.759
By convention, the N-terminus is listed as
the first amino acid in the chain, and the

00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:50.390
C-terminus is last.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:57.500
Let’s select these, rename them termini,
and then zoom.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:02.380
Now show the termini as sticks: S, sticks.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:08.179
Here’s the amine of the N-terminus, and
here’s the carboxylic acid group of the

00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:09.450
C-terminus.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:15.070
Notice in the sequence, Chain B has a bunch
of grayed out amino acids that can’t be

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.290
seen in the structure below.

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:22.430
Sometimes, a crystallographer will list them
because they were present in the protein that

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:28.610
was crystallized but didn’t show up well
enough in the crystal structure to give 3D

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:29.940
coordinates for.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:37.030
Let’s zoom back out, and then manually zoom
in on the interface with the trackpad or mouse.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:42.760
Notice amino acids near the interacting region
are automatically shown as sticks with this

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:43.760
preset.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:44.760
Convenient.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:51.060
Let’s see if there are some polar contacts:
ionic interactions and/or hydrogen bonds.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:57.010
A, find, polar contacts, between chains.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.420
And there they are.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:02.890
The group of polar contacts shows up in the
names panel.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:09.750
If we find the measurements distracting, we
can hide the labels, and just show the lines.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:15.930
Polar contacts aren’t the only stabilizing
interactions at protein interfaces or in binding

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:17.390
sites.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:22.750
Van der Waals interactions, like induced dipoles,
contribute as well.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:27.340
And nonpolar chains cluster together in the
hydrophobic effect.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:32.370
To see if these interactions are present,
we have to manually measure.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:36.010
Using the dropdown Wizard menu, choose measurement.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:42.989
Now we’ll manually measure between two nonpolar
side chains—ones that only contain carbon

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:44.349
atoms.

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:49.909
The measurement wizard pops up just above
the mouse control menu, and this prompt in

00:03:49.909 --> 00:03:55.010
the upper left of the structure viewer directs
us to click on the first atom.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:57.950
Let’s look for some nonpolar interactions.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:03.269
Here’s a valine side chain that looks pretty
close to this isoleucine.

00:04:03.269 --> 00:04:09.760
As long as the measurement is about 4.4 angstroms
or less, it’s likely these surfaces are

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:10.950
interacting.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:16.750
Let’s pick the carbon on the valine that
appears to be closest to the isoleucine.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:22.230
Now, we’ll measure to this carbon on isoleucine.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:26.820
4.2—that’s a likely interaction.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:33.380
Let’s see if there are any others.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:37.260
This phenylalanine is pretty close to this
tyrosine.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:43.570
Check this out, tyrosine has a polar contact—a
hydrogen bond to glutamate right here.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:48.400
Because of the oxygen, it can make polar interactions.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.780
But it’s got a big carbon ring too.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.550
Let’s measure between these two rings.

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:59.190
And yup, 3.9—likely some hydrophobic clustering
going on there.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:04.220
A quick way to identify whether an amino acid
side chain can make polar interactions is

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.100
to look for blue or red in the chain.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:12.170
Nitrogen and oxygen are polar atoms, and can
make polar contacts.

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:17.480
But any side chain with a lot of carbon atoms
can make favorable van der Waals interactions

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:18.569
too.

00:05:18.569 --> 00:05:23.630
The tyrosine side chain we looked at is a
great example of a side chain that can participate

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:26.120
in both types of interactions.

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:30.290
I’ll finish this lesson with a quick word
of caution.

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:35.360
Sometimes the more granular find polar contacts
commands don’t work as expected.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:40.400
I suspect it’s the way the information is
coded in some PDB files.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:45.510
But, if you don’t see any contacts at all
when selecting something like “just intra

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:52.700
main chain”, double check that by selecting
polar contacts “to any atoms”.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:54.590
And that’s a wrap on this lesson.

00:05:54.590 --> 00:06:00.090
Join me next time when we take a deep dive
into secondary structure and learn more about

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:01.850
the measurement wizard settings.

